## 🚀 Advanced API Fuzzing Playbooks## 1. The Method-Overriding & Endpoint Discovery Matrix
99% of hunters miss API endpoints because they only check them with a GET request. If an endpoint requires a POST or PUT, it may return a 404 or 405, fooling you into thinking it doesn't exist. This command forces ffuf to dynamically test every path against every HTTP method simultaneously.

ffuf -w /path/to/SecLists/Discovery/Web-Content/api/api-endpoints.txt:ROUTE \
     -w /path/to/SecLists/Discovery/Web-Content/web-vulnerabilities.txt:METHOD \
     -u https://target.com \
     -X METHOD \
     -mode clusterbomb \
     -mc all -fc 404 -ignore-body -v


* 
* The Logic: By using -mode clusterbomb, ffuf tests every path with GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, and PATCH.
* The Magic: -mc all -fc 404 hides the dead routes but alerts you to anything else. If /users returns a 405 Method Not Allowed on a GET request but a 415 Unsupported Media Type on a POST request, you just found a live API injection point.
* 

## 2. Mass Content-Type Brute Forcing (Bypassing JSON Filters)
Many APIs use restrictive input filters. If you send data as application/json, it might get blocked by a WAF. However, if you fuzz the Content-Type header, you can sometimes trigger an application fallback that processes other data types unvalidated.

ffuf -w /path/to/SecLists/Miscellaneous/web-content-types.txt:CTYPE \
     -u https://target.com \
     -X POST \
     -H "Content-Type: CTYPE" \
     -d '{"admin": true, "user_id": "1002"}' \
     -ac


* 
* The Magic: This bypasses front-end parsing layers. You might find that the API engine rejects JSON input but blindly accepts and processes data when the header is switched to application/xml or application/x-yaml, potentially introducing serious parsing vulnerabilities.
* 

## 3. Unlinked & Hidden API Parameter Hunting
Modern REST and GraphQL APIs often accept undocumented parameters used exclusively for internal debugging, feature-flag testing, or legacy compatibility.

ffuf -w /path/to/SecLists/Discovery/Web-Content/burp-parameter-names.txt \
     -u https://target.com \
     -H "Authorization: Bearer [JWT_TOKEN]" \
     -ac -acc "status,size"


* 
* The Magic: By utilizing strict auto-calibration (-ac -acc "status,size"), ffuf filters out the baseline response of the checkout page. If a parameter name like &debug=true, &admin=1, or &testing=true changes the byte size or response structure even slightly, you've exposed an unlinked business logic trigger.
* 

